{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "01-beta-reduction.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "β-reduction",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Reduce λ-terms to normal form by β-reduction, and see why substitution must rename bound variables.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Terms and β-reduction\n> A term is a variable $x$, a function $\\lambda x.\\, M$, or an application $M\\ N$. A **redex** is a function applied to an argument, $(\\lambda x.\\, M)\\ N$, and β-reduction replaces it by $M[x := N]$: the body with the argument put in for the parameter.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Type λ as `\\lam` then space (or a backslash). The engine reduces in **normal order**: always the leftmost, outermost redex first.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(λx. x) y",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(λx. x x) (λy. y)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "(λx. λy. x) a b",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Normal forms",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Normal form\n> A term with no redex left is in **normal form**. Two terms are equal as programs when they reduce to the same normal form, up to the names of bound variables ($\\lambda x.\\, x$ and $\\lambda y.\\, y$ are the same function).",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The exercises use exactly this: your answer and the question are both reduced, and compared by their normal forms. So any name for a bound variable is right.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(λx. λy. y x) a (λz. z)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce to normal form.",
      "hints": [
        "The first redex puts $a$ for $x$: $(\\lambda y.\\, y\\ a)\\ (\\lambda z.\\, z)$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(λx. x x) (λy. y)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce to normal form. Any name for the bound variable will do.",
      "hints": [
        "$x$ is replaced by $\\lambda y.\\, y$ twice: $(\\lambda y.\\, y)(\\lambda y.\\, y)$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Capture",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake]\n> Substituting blindly can **capture** a variable. In $(\\lambda x.\\, \\lambda y.\\, x)\\ y$, putting $y$ for $x$ naively gives $\\lambda y.\\, y$, the identity, but the $y$ that was passed in is free, not the parameter. The engine renames the binder first (an α-step) and gets $\\lambda y'.\\, y$, the function that ignores its argument and returns $y$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(λx. λy. x) y",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(λx. λy. x) y",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $(\\lambda x.\\, \\lambda y.\\, x)\\ y$ to normal form. (The trap: $\\lambda y.\\, y$ is wrong.)",
      "hints": [
        "Rename the inner binder first, say to $z$: $\\lambda x.\\, \\lambda z.\\, x$."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Computation in the λ-calculus is substitution: find the leftmost outermost redex, put the argument in for the parameter, renaming binders that would capture, and repeat until nothing is left to reduce.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
